<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:16:Protected Mode:default">
<!--
    kuna `signedness`: the width below which a declaration is never re-signed is
    the `int` of the compiler that reads kuna's output, not the `int` of the
    target.

    C promotes anything narrower than `int` before every operator, and the
    declaration decides which extension that is, so below the promotion width
    even `==` and `!=` are signedness-sensitive: `(short)-1 == -1` is true while
    `(unsigned short)0xffff == -1` is a constant false.  The sibling test
    tests/stages/kuna-signedness.xml pins that rule on x86-64.  This one pins it
    on a target whose own cspec says `integer_size` is 2 (the same holds for
    avr8gcc, avr8egcc, TI_MSP430, TI_MSP430X, CR16 and PIC24): reading the guard
    off the target would let a 2-byte local be re-signed there, and the emitted
    `if (v1 != -1)` would become a constant-true test to every reader whose `int`
    is 4 bytes.

    `f` at 0x0 is the guarded case, a 2-byte local that nothing settles:

        0000  be 00 01        MOV  SI,0x100
        0003  8b 04           MOV  AX,[SI]        ; v1, 2 bytes wide
        0005  8b 5c 02        MOV  BX,[SI + 0x2]
        0008  83 f8 ff        CMP  AX,-0x1
        000b  74 05           JZ   0x12
        000d  01 d8           ADD  AX,BX
        000f  89 04           MOV  [SI],AX
        0011  c3              RET
        0012  31 c0           XOR  AX,AX
        0014  c3              RET

    `g` at 0x100 is the control: the same situation one width up, where the
    reader's promotions do not run and the option is free to act.

        0100  be 00 02        MOV  SI,0x200
        0103  66 8b 04        MOV  EAX,[SI]       ; v1, 4 bytes wide
        0106  66 01 c0        ADD  EAX,EAX
        0109  66 89 04        MOV  [SI],EAX
        010c  66 89 44 04     MOV  [SI + 0x4],EAX
        0110  66 89 44 08     MOV  [SI + 0x8],EAX
        0114  c3              RET

    Stage: P9 emission, sub-stage "naming-policy".  P0 assertion: `option
    signedness upstream|auto|prefer-signed|prefer-unsigned`.

    Asserts 1 and 2 are the guard: `prefer-unsigned`, the arm with the loosest
    tie-break, leaves the 2-byte declaration exactly as upstream wrote it.  Pass
    1 and pass 3 name `option signedness upstream` explicitly, so each arm is
    compared against the declaration type inference produces and not against
    whichever value happens to be the shipped default.
    Asserts 3 and 4 are the control that makes 1 and 2 mean something: the same
    arm does re-sign the 4-byte local in `g` on this same target, so the decline
    above is the width rule and not an inactive option.

    Assert 1 matches the declaration by SHAPE (`int2 v<n>;`, no pointer star)
    rather than by name: which `v<n>` the 2-byte value gets depends on how many
    locals are declared ahead of it, which is not this test's subject.  The star
    is what keeps that honest, because a pointer local in `f` is spelled
    `int2 *v<n>;` and does not match.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x0">
be00018b048b5c0283f8ff740501d88904c331c0c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100">
be0002668b046601c06689046689440466894408c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x0" name="f"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100" name="g"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option signedness upstream</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option signedness prefer-unsigned</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option signedness upstream</com>
  <com>lo fu g</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option signedness prefer-unsigned</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="signedness-int16 #1: the 2-byte local keeps its declaration under both arms, because the reader promotes it before every operator (once per pass)" min="2" max="2">^  int2 v[0-9]+;$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="signedness-int16 #2: nothing 2 bytes wide is ever re-signed on this target, whose own int is 2 bytes" min="0" max="0">^  uint2 </stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="signedness-int16 #3: the 4-byte control is declared signed under option signedness upstream (once)" min="1" max="1">^  int4 v1;$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="signedness-int16 #4: and prefer-unsigned does re-sign it on this same target, so #1 is the width rule declining and not an inactive option (once)" min="1" max="1">^  uint4 v1;$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
